\chapter{Numerics, forecasting, and data-search kernels}
\label{ch:105}
\index{Numerics, forecasting, and data-search kernels}

The numerical inventory has families for scalar operations, data preparation, search, and forecasting. Do not infer callable On1x support for an entire family from one reviewed adapter. The pricing example uses documented source profiles for selected historical-price and optimization calls. More general numerical APIs can carry arrays, mutable state, optional errors and shape constraints that need separate transfer and validation rules.

\section{Verify a deterministic data path}
Construct a tiny ordered history with an expected summary and a case that reaches an out-of-domain status. Run it through the exact reviewed fixture path. Check representation and error treatment at each boundary before using a larger data set. When comparing a prediction algorithm, keep time order, units, missing values and rounding explicit. For a data-search kernel, compare exact expected matches including duplicates and boundary positions. Measure generalization or performance separately from the correctness of the C11 ABI adapter.

\section{Check the candidate decision by hand}
The pricing program embeds amounts in minor units. Its conversion
checks a nonnegative bound of $2^{53}$ before converting the integer
and dividing by 100. The resulting five observations are 10, 12,
14, 16 and 18. Only the first three go into the training slice.
The helper compares the one-step forecasts against 16 by squared
error; the program expects Holt to be selected. Its separate two-step
forecast is compared against 18. If the training slice inadvertently
included 16, these comparisons would no longer test forecasting from
the same origin. Unavailable variance is carried as NaN: comparing a
value to itself for inequality in the example detects this case rather
than reporting a measured variance of zero.

For the discrete decision, start at index one in the price array so
that the leading 999 is excluded. Pair prices 10, 12 and 15 with
demands 12, 9 and 4. With unit cost four and the profit objective,
the candidate scores are $(10-4)12=72$, $(12-4)9=72$ and
$(15-4)4=44$. Thus the best score is shared; checking only that
the winner beats the last candidate is a weak oracle. On equal scores
the kernel chooses the lower price. Expect price 10, expected demand
12, score 72, and three evaluations. Reordering the paired observations
should leave that decision unchanged.

Shorten just the demand slice to two entries. Both slices still have
individually valid bounds, but there is no third demand to pair with
the third price. The example expects the Rejected arm, not a partial
decision over the shorter list. This is a relationship between inputs
that no single slice capacity check can express.

